    a: "Fast tracking compresses the schedule by performing activities that were originally planned sequentially in parallel instead — it doesn't cost more money directly, but it increases risk (e.g. starting construction before design is fully finalized risks rework if design changes) and can only be applied where dependencies aren't strictly mandatory. Crashing compresses the schedule by adding more resources to critical-path activities (more people, overtime, expediting fees) — it costs more money and has diminishing/negative returns past a point (Brooks's Law: adding people to an already-late project can make it later due to onboarding and coordination overhead). Both only help if applied to activities actually ON the critical path.",
