    a: "RAID stands for Risks, Assumptions, Issues, and Dependencies — a single consolidated log tracking all four related-but-distinct categories of project uncertainty and constraint in one place, rather than in separate documents. A standalone risk register only tracks risks (potential future events); a RAID log additionally tracks assumptions (things believed true but unverified — which, if wrong, can themselves become risks), issues (things that have ALREADY happened and need active resolution, as opposed to risks which haven't happened yet), and dependencies (reliance on other teams/projects/vendors). Many PMs favor a RAID log specifically because these four categories interact constantly (an unvalidated assumption becoming an issue, a dependency slipping and creating a new risk) and tracking them together surfaces those interactions better than four disconnected documents would.",
