    a: "Qualitative risk analysis assesses and prioritizes risks using relative, descriptive scales (e.g. probability and impact each rated Low/Medium/High, or a 1-5 scale) rather than precise numerical modeling — it's fast, doesn't require detailed data, and is usually done for every risk in the register as a first pass. A probability-impact matrix plots each risk's probability rating against its impact rating in a grid, with predefined zones (e.g. combinations landing in the top-right corner are 'high priority,' bottom-left are 'low priority, monitor only') — it gives a fast, visual, defensible way to decide which risks deserve deeper (quantitative) analysis and active response planning versus which can just sit on a watchlist.",
