    a: "Quantitative risk analysis numerically models the combined effect of risks on overall project objectives — techniques include Expected Monetary Value (EMV = probability × impact, in dollars), decision tree analysis (mapping out sequential decision points and their probability-weighted outcomes), and Monte Carlo simulation (running thousands of randomized scenarios across the schedule/cost model, using probability distributions for each activity's duration/cost, to produce a confidence-level distribution of possible project outcomes rather than one single-point estimate). It's typically reserved for large, high-stakes, or high-uncertainty projects where the extra rigor and effort are justified by the size of the decision riding on it (e.g. setting a contingency reserve that's actually defensible to a sponsor) — most smaller projects stop at qualitative analysis.",
